  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Cangoronin may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through multiple pathways. Cangoronin is shown to have a target of HEp-2, which is a cell line that is targeted by several compounds, including (1R,2S,3R,7S,9R,14R,16R,17R)-3,16-dihydroxy-14-[(2S,3R)-3-(hydroxymethyl)-5-oxooxolan-2-yl]-2,6,14,17-tetramethyl-10-oxatetracyclo[7.7.1.02,7.013,17]heptadeca-5,12-diene-4,11-dione and (5S)-4-Bromo-3-[(1R)-1-Hydroxybutyl]-5-(Iodomethyl)-5-Methoxyfuran-2-One. Both of these compounds are known to target Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase, indicating a potential mechanism of action involving this enzyme. Additionally, HEp-2 is also targeted by 2,6-Dimethyl-2-heptenal, which also has a known target of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The scores for these relationships are consistent, suggesting a plausible and well-supported interaction between Cangoronin and Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The evidence is robust and aligns across different pathways, supporting a clear and direct relationship between the two entities.",
